lated.", "Incumbent Wes Moore (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Wes Moore seeks a second term against Dan Cox, the same Republican he defeated statewide in 2022. Maryland's Democratic lean, Moore's incumbency, and a vast financial edge — more than $15 million raised versus about $240,000 for Cox — frame the contest. Every major race rating places Maryland in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and betting markets give Moore roughly a 96 to 98 percent chance of winning.\n\nThe only recent independent poll, from early August, showed Moore ahead 58 to 31 among likely voters. Cox has campaigned on electricity costs, data centers, and redistricting, and the candidates meet in debates in October, but nothing in the public record suggests the race is competitive. The Green and independent candidates raise negligible money and are not factors. The realistic question is the size of Moore's winning margin, not the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 19.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9967, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9939, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9968, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9958, "margin": 21.0}]}